1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,351
Video games are an industry that eats its kings.

2
00:00:03,631 --> 00:00:06,209
Atari defined the home console—and collapsed.

3
00:00:06,489 --> 00:00:09,560
Sega challenged the world—and left the hardware business.

4
00:00:09,840 --> 00:00:16,920
Entire generations of studios, machines, and billion-dollar strategies have arrived, dominated, and disappeared.

5
00:00:17,640 --> 00:00:19,290
But one company keeps coming back.

6
00:00:19,570 --> 00:00:22,065
It survived the crash of the American games market.

7
00:00:22,345 --> 00:00:23,402
It survived Sony.

8
00:00:23,682 --> 00:00:25,015
It survived Microsoft.

9
00:00:25,295 --> 00:00:27,060
It survived its own worst console.

10
00:00:27,340 --> 00:00:31,641
And it did this while routinely selling less powerful hardware than everyone else.

11
00:00:32,361 --> 00:00:36,103
Nintendo began in 1889, making playing cards in Kyoto.

12
00:00:36,383 --> 00:00:39,449
More than a century later, it remains one of the most important

13
00:00:39,449 --> 00:00:41,055
entertainment companies on Earth.

14
00:00:41,775 --> 00:00:43,107
The obvious question is how.

15
00:00:43,387 --> 00:00:48,224
The better question is: what does Nintendo understand about play that its competitors keep forgetting?

16
00:00:53,144 --> 00:00:57,500
Nintendo's history is often told as a parade of beloved things:

17
00:00:57,500 --> 00:00:59,989
Mario, Zelda, Pokémon, the Game Boy,

18
00:00:59,989 --> 00:01:01,372
the Wii, the Switch.

19
00:01:01,652 --> 00:01:02,307
That is true.

20
00:01:02,587 --> 00:01:03,723
It is also incomplete.

21
00:01:04,443 --> 00:01:08,467
Nintendo has spent much of its life making decisions that looked wrong at the time.

22
00:01:08,747 --> 00:01:12,911
The Game Boy's screen was monochrome when color competitors already existed.

23
00:01:13,191 --> 00:01:15,518
The Wii ignored the high-definition arms race.

24
00:01:15,798 --> 00:01:20,290
The Switch launched with mobile-class hardware into a world of 4K consoles.

25
00:01:21,010 --> 00:01:22,810
And Nintendo has failed—badly.

26
00:01:23,090 --> 00:01:24,607
The Virtual Boy was abandoned.

27
00:01:24,887 --> 00:01:27,319
The GameCube finished behind its main rivals.

28
00:01:27,599 --> 00:01:31,007
The Wii U sold only 13.56 million units.

29
00:01:31,727 --> 00:01:38,411
Yet by September 2026, the original Switch family had sold more than 157 million systems.

30
00:01:38,691 --> 00:01:42,354
Switch 2 sold almost 20 million in its first fiscal year.

31
00:01:42,634 --> 00:01:49,281
Nintendo's financial year ending March 2026 produced 2.31 trillion yen in net sales.

32
00:01:50,001 --> 00:01:52,527
This is not a story of uninterrupted genius.

33
00:01:52,807 --> 00:01:56,032
It is a story about a company with an unusually effective way

34
00:01:56,032 --> 00:01:57,672
of recovering from being wrong.

35
00:02:00,692 --> 00:02:03,146
Most technology companies begin with a machine.

36
00:02:03,426 --> 00:02:05,507
Nintendo often begins with an action.

37
00:02:06,227 --> 00:02:08,002
What if a game continued in your pocket?

38
00:02:08,282 --> 00:02:09,932
What if you aimed by moving your hand?

39
00:02:10,212 --> 00:02:12,758
What if two people could play by splitting one controller?

40
00:02:13,038 --> 00:02:15,823
What if a console could follow you from the television to the

41
00:02:15,823 --> 00:02:17,193
train without ending the game?

42
00:02:17,473 --> 00:02:18,983
That distinction sounds small.

43
00:02:19,263 --> 00:02:20,304
It changes everything.

44
00:02:21,024 --> 00:02:26,232
When Nintendo entered electronic toys, its great advantage was not semiconductor expertise.

45
00:02:26,512 --> 00:02:28,451
It was the mentality of a toy maker:

46
00:02:28,451 --> 00:02:30,982
the product had to explain itself through play.

47
00:02:31,702 --> 00:02:35,136
The Game & Watch did not ask people to imagine a portable computer.

48
00:02:35,416 --> 00:02:37,160
It put one tiny game in a pocket.

49
00:02:37,440 --> 00:02:39,728
Donkey Kong did not sell processing power.

50
00:02:40,008 --> 00:02:43,631
It gave players a story they could understand in seconds:

51
00:02:43,631 --> 00:02:46,810
a carpenter, a captive, a giant ape, and a ladder.

52
00:02:47,530 --> 00:02:52,021
Shigeru Miyamoto's great contribution was not merely inventing characters.

53
00:02:52,301 --> 00:02:53,832
It was making mechanics readable.

54
00:02:54,112 --> 00:02:58,939
Mario's jump, Link's sword, the ink in Splatoon—each tells the player what

55
00:02:58,939 --> 00:03:00,047
the world is for.

56
00:03:00,767 --> 00:03:05,450
Nintendo calls much of this “integrated hardware and software.” But the corporate

57
00:03:05,450 --> 00:03:06,780
phrase hides the magic.

58
00:03:07,060 --> 00:03:12,974
A Nintendo machine is strongest when one physical gesture and one digital response feel inseparable.

59
00:03:13,694 --> 00:03:17,646
The DS had two screens because Nintendo wanted new kinds of attention.

60
00:03:17,926 --> 00:03:21,179
The Wii had motion controls because it wanted new kinds of players.

61
00:03:21,459 --> 00:03:23,292
The Switch had rails, a kickstand,

62
00:03:23,292 --> 00:03:27,766
and detachable controllers because it wanted play to move between rooms and people.

63
00:03:28,486 --> 00:03:31,164
Competitors asked: how much more can the box do?

64
00:03:31,444 --> 00:03:35,240
Nintendo asked: what can the player do now that they could not do before?

65
00:03:38,260 --> 00:03:43,639
This philosophy has a name, associated with legendary Nintendo designer Gunpei Yokoi:

66
00:03:43,639 --> 00:03:46,297
lateral thinking with withered technology.

67
00:03:46,577 --> 00:03:52,372
Use mature, inexpensive, well-understood technology—and combine it in a way nobody expects.

68
00:03:53,092 --> 00:03:55,641
The original Game Boy is the perfect example.

69
00:03:55,921 --> 00:03:58,936
In 1989, it was not the most advanced handheld.

70
00:03:59,216 --> 00:04:00,525
Its screen had no color.

71
00:04:00,805 --> 00:04:02,868
It looked almost stubbornly old-fashioned.

72
00:04:03,148 --> 00:04:05,372
But older technology meant lower cost,

73
00:04:05,372 --> 00:04:08,533
longer battery life, and hardware Nintendo understood.

74
00:04:08,813 --> 00:04:12,654
Then Tetris turned those limitations into a mass-market machine.

75
00:04:13,374 --> 00:04:14,521
The Wii repeated the trick.

76
00:04:14,801 --> 00:04:19,569
Sony and Microsoft competed over high-definition graphics and expensive processors.

77
00:04:19,849 --> 00:04:24,254
Nintendo used modest hardware and a motion-sensitive remote that looked familiar to

78
00:04:24,254 --> 00:04:26,005
anyone who had used a television.

79
00:04:26,285 --> 00:04:28,503
The result was not merely a cheaper console.

80
00:04:28,783 --> 00:04:29,769
It was an invitation.

81
00:04:30,049 --> 00:04:34,665
Parents, grandparents, and people who would never memorize twelve buttons could understand

82
00:04:34,665 --> 00:04:37,024
bowling before the first ball left their hand.

83
00:04:37,744 --> 00:04:42,443
This is why judging Nintendo by technical specifications so often misses the point.

84
00:04:42,723 --> 00:04:47,420
Power is useful, but power is easily copied and eventually becomes ordinary.

85
00:04:47,700 --> 00:04:48,964
A new behavior is harder.

86
00:04:49,244 --> 00:04:52,554
It requires hardware, software, interface, price,

87
00:04:52,554 --> 00:04:54,446
and timing to land together.

88
00:04:55,166 --> 00:04:58,325
Nintendo's innovation is rarely invention from nothing.

89
00:04:58,605 --> 00:05:03,906
It is recombination: old components, rearranged around a fresh human experience.

90
00:05:06,926 --> 00:05:08,139
Hardware creates a moment.

91
00:05:08,419 --> 00:05:10,552
Characters carry that moment across time.

92
00:05:11,272 --> 00:05:14,957
Mario is not valuable only because people recognize his face.

93
00:05:15,237 --> 00:05:16,739
Mario is a memory machine.

94
00:05:17,019 --> 00:05:21,338
For one generation, he is the first secret block in Super Mario

95
00:05:21,338 --> 00:05:24,218
Bros. For another, the castle in Mario 64.

96
00:05:24,498 --> 00:05:26,981
For another, a family argument on Rainbow Road.

97
00:05:27,701 --> 00:05:32,685
Nintendo's franchises survive because they are consistent in feeling but flexible in form.

98
00:05:32,965 --> 00:05:34,980
Zelda can be a top-down puzzle,

99
00:05:34,980 --> 00:05:37,841
a 3D adventure, a motion-control experiment,

100
00:05:37,841 --> 00:05:41,482
or an open world built around physics and improvisation.

101
00:05:41,762 --> 00:05:44,524
Mario can be a platformer, a racing game,

102
00:05:44,524 --> 00:05:47,555
a party game, a role-playing game, or a film.

103
00:05:47,835 --> 00:05:49,368
The character remains familiar.

104
00:05:49,648 --> 00:05:50,888
The activity changes.

105
00:05:51,608 --> 00:05:52,778
That balance compounds.

106
00:05:53,058 --> 00:05:55,121
Each successful game sells the next one.

107
00:05:55,401 --> 00:06:00,334
Each console arrives with a library of emotional associations already waiting for it.

108
00:06:00,614 --> 00:06:03,579
Parents who once received a Game Boy can give their children a

109
00:06:03,579 --> 00:06:05,635
Switch without needing the brand explained.

110
00:06:06,355 --> 00:06:10,625
This is also why Nintendo protects its intellectual property so aggressively.

111
00:06:10,905 --> 00:06:12,688
The legal posture can feel severe.

112
00:06:12,968 --> 00:06:18,337
But strategically, the company is guarding the continuity between generations—the belief that

113
00:06:18,337 --> 00:06:20,993
a Mario experience will still feel like Mario.

114
00:06:21,713 --> 00:06:24,465
And the loop now extends beyond consoles:

115
00:06:24,465 --> 00:06:30,640
films, theme parks, stores, museums, and merchandise create more doors into the same worlds.

116
00:06:30,920 --> 00:06:34,221
Nintendo is no longer using games to support hardware alone.

117
00:06:34,501 --> 00:06:38,833
It is using hardware, games, and physical experiences to keep a universe

118
00:06:38,833 --> 00:06:40,699
of characters culturally alive.

119
00:06:43,719 --> 00:06:46,514
But this system can fail when the action is not clear.

120
00:06:46,794 --> 00:06:48,455
The Wii U had a controller with a screen.

121
00:06:48,735 --> 00:06:52,976
It offered asymmetric multiplayer, off-TV play, and clever ideas.

122
00:06:53,256 --> 00:06:55,550
But the name sounded like an accessory for the Wii.

123
00:06:55,830 --> 00:06:59,030
The marketing struggled to explain why the second screen mattered.

124
00:06:59,310 --> 00:07:00,630
Third-party support weakened.

125
00:07:00,910 --> 00:07:02,469
Software arrived too slowly.

126
00:07:03,189 --> 00:07:05,407
The Wii had been understood in a single swing.

127
00:07:05,687 --> 00:07:07,192
The Wii U required a paragraph.

128
00:07:07,912 --> 00:07:11,317
It became Nintendo's worst-selling home console,

129
00:07:11,317 --> 00:07:13,090
with 13.56 million units.

130
00:07:13,370 --> 00:07:16,140
In the fiscal year ending March 2014,

131
00:07:16,140 --> 00:07:18,835
Nintendo reported an operating loss.

132
00:07:19,555 --> 00:07:23,993
This is the point where a conventional technology company might retreat toward

133
00:07:23,993 --> 00:07:26,782
the industry standard: build a more powerful box,

134
00:07:26,782 --> 00:07:30,025
copy the successful interface, compete on familiar terms.

135
00:07:30,305 --> 00:07:32,095
Nintendo did something more revealing.

136
00:07:32,375 --> 00:07:35,581
It kept the core insight and discarded the confusing product.

137
00:07:36,301 --> 00:07:37,791
Off-TV play had been useful.

138
00:07:38,071 --> 00:07:40,771
A personal screen near the television had been useful.

139
00:07:41,051 --> 00:07:44,689
Local multiplayer built around unusual controllers had been useful.

140
00:07:44,969 --> 00:07:47,824
The problem was that these ideas were trapped in a machine that

141
00:07:47,824 --> 00:07:48,958
could not leave the room.

142
00:07:49,678 --> 00:07:51,299
So Nintendo removed the room.

143
00:07:54,319 --> 00:07:56,296
The Switch was not a rejection of the Wii U.

144
00:07:56,576 --> 00:07:58,809
It was the Wii U's best idea, completed.

145
00:07:59,089 --> 00:07:59,699
One screen.

146
00:07:59,979 --> 00:08:01,514
Two detachable controllers.

147
00:08:01,794 --> 00:08:05,186
A dock that made the transition to television almost invisible.

148
00:08:05,466 --> 00:08:09,491
And a reveal trailer that explained the entire product without a technical lecture.

149
00:08:10,211 --> 00:08:10,827
Play at home.

150
00:08:11,107 --> 00:08:11,969
Lift the console.

151
00:08:12,249 --> 00:08:12,877
Keep playing.

152
00:08:13,157 --> 00:08:14,023
Share a controller.

153
00:08:14,303 --> 00:08:14,922
Play together.

154
00:08:15,642 --> 00:08:19,368
The machine unified Nintendo's handheld and home-console development.

155
00:08:19,648 --> 00:08:22,680
Instead of splitting major games between two platforms,

156
00:08:22,680 --> 00:08:25,270
Nintendo could concentrate its software on one.

157
00:08:25,990 --> 00:08:28,161
Then came the crucial part: the games.

158
00:08:28,441 --> 00:08:31,698
The Legend of Zelda: Breath of the Wild made the launch feel

159
00:08:31,698 --> 00:08:32,838
like a new beginning.

160
00:08:33,118 --> 00:08:36,653
Mario Kart 8 Deluxe turned an overlooked Wii U game into one

161
00:08:36,653 --> 00:08:39,481
of the best-selling games in Nintendo's history.

162
00:08:39,761 --> 00:08:44,333
Animal Crossing: New Horizons arrived at the exact moment millions of people

163
00:08:44,333 --> 00:08:46,559
needed a gentle place to be together.

164
00:08:47,279 --> 00:08:50,001
The Switch did not win because it had the best graphics.

165
00:08:50,281 --> 00:08:53,400
It won because the proposition remained useful for years:

166
00:08:53,400 --> 00:08:55,370
this is your console, your handheld,

167
00:08:55,370 --> 00:08:58,543
and sometimes the thing you hand to the person beside you.

168
00:08:59,263 --> 00:09:04,221
By September 2026, Switch hardware sales had passed 157 million,

169
00:09:04,221 --> 00:09:07,630
with nearly 1.6 billion software units sold.

170
00:09:08,350 --> 00:09:13,415
That recovery—from 13.56 million Wii U systems to more than 157 million

171
00:09:13,415 --> 00:09:18,124
Switch systems—is not evidence that Nintendo never makes mistakes.

172
00:09:18,404 --> 00:09:22,355
It is evidence that Nintendo knows how to salvage an insight from a mistake.

173
00:09:25,375 --> 00:09:27,469
There is another reason Nintendo survives,

174
00:09:27,469 --> 00:09:29,613
and it is harder to place in a spreadsheet.

175
00:09:29,893 --> 00:09:31,482
Nintendo designs for delight.

176
00:09:31,762 --> 00:09:33,101
Not happiness as branding.

177
00:09:33,381 --> 00:09:35,322
Delight as a system of feedback:

178
00:09:35,322 --> 00:09:38,475
the sound of a coin, the pause before a chest opens,

179
00:09:38,475 --> 00:09:41,022
the tiny animation that rewards curiosity,

180
00:09:41,022 --> 00:09:45,267
the possibility that a piano might secretly be a character with teeth.

181
00:09:45,987 --> 00:09:47,946
These details have economic value.

182
00:09:48,226 --> 00:09:52,311
A game built around spectacle can become dated when better spectacle arrives.

183
00:09:52,591 --> 00:09:54,782
A game built around a satisfying jump,

184
00:09:54,782 --> 00:09:59,280
a readable rule, or the pleasure of discovery can remain playable for decades.

185
00:10:00,000 --> 00:10:04,108
Nintendo's best software is polished around the player's body and attention.

186
00:10:04,388 --> 00:10:06,514
Controls are tested until they disappear.

187
00:10:06,794 --> 00:10:09,393
Levels teach without stopping to announce the lesson.

188
00:10:09,673 --> 00:10:13,268
Failure is often funny enough to make another attempt feel inviting.

189
00:10:13,988 --> 00:10:18,476
That accessibility expands the audience without necessarily flattening the design.

190
00:10:18,756 --> 00:10:21,033
A child can finish a lap in Mario Kart.

191
00:10:21,313 --> 00:10:23,922
An expert can spend years mastering its lines.

192
00:10:24,202 --> 00:10:26,053
A newcomer can wander through Hyrule.

193
00:10:26,333 --> 00:10:29,727
A speedrunner can bend its physics into a different game entirely.

194
00:10:30,447 --> 00:10:33,706
“Fun for everyone” is easy to dismiss as a slogan.

195
00:10:33,986 --> 00:10:37,063
At Nintendo, it is closer to an engineering constraint.

196
00:10:40,083 --> 00:10:43,978
None of this makes Nintendo infallible—or universally benevolent.

197
00:10:44,258 --> 00:10:47,417
Its online services have often lagged behind competitors.

198
00:10:47,697 --> 00:10:51,837
Its approach to preservation can make older games difficult to access legally.

199
00:10:52,117 --> 00:10:54,268
Hardware experiments create casualties.

200
00:10:54,548 --> 00:10:58,777
Fans and creators have criticized its litigation and strict control of its work.

201
00:10:59,057 --> 00:11:02,545
And even beloved products can carry frustrating design flaws.

202
00:11:03,265 --> 00:11:06,149
Nintendo's strategy also produces volatility.

203
00:11:06,429 --> 00:11:09,653
When an unusual machine connects, it can expand the market.

204
00:11:09,933 --> 00:11:12,109
When it does not, there is no safe middle.

205
00:11:12,389 --> 00:11:15,460
The same independence that creates the Wii can create the Wii U.

206
00:11:16,180 --> 00:11:17,579
And the industry is changing.

207
00:11:17,859 --> 00:11:19,456
Development costs are rising.

208
00:11:19,736 --> 00:11:22,209
Mobile games compete for every idle minute.

209
00:11:22,489 --> 00:11:27,520
Younger players gather inside persistent platforms that behave less like individual games

210
00:11:27,520 --> 00:11:29,102
and more like social spaces.

211
00:11:29,822 --> 00:11:32,446
Nintendo cannot survive on nostalgia alone.

212
00:11:32,726 --> 00:11:36,702
Nostalgia is powerful, but it is a diminishing resource if a new

213
00:11:36,702 --> 00:11:39,497
generation does not form memories of its own.

214
00:11:39,777 --> 00:11:44,593
The real test of each Nintendo platform is therefore not whether old players return.

215
00:11:44,873 --> 00:11:47,949
It is whether new players find an experience they will still care

216
00:11:47,949 --> 00:11:49,274
about twenty years from now.

217
00:11:52,294 --> 00:11:56,021
So why has Nintendo endured where competitors shrank or disappeared?

218
00:11:56,301 --> 00:11:58,912
Because most advantages in technology decay.

219
00:11:59,192 --> 00:12:01,116
The fastest processor becomes cheap.

220
00:12:01,396 --> 00:12:03,462
A manufacturing edge becomes standard.

221
00:12:03,742 --> 00:12:05,600
A distribution channel gets replaced.

222
00:12:05,880 --> 00:12:07,032
A hit game is copied.

223
00:12:07,752 --> 00:12:10,994
Nintendo built three advantages that reinforce one another.

224
00:12:11,274 --> 00:12:14,608
First, a design culture that searches for new forms of play.

225
00:12:14,888 --> 00:12:18,543
Second, control over the hardware and software needed to deliver them.

226
00:12:18,823 --> 00:12:23,264
Third, characters that make each new experiment feel familiar enough to try.

227
00:12:23,984 --> 00:12:26,681
Remove any one of these and the system weakens.

228
00:12:26,961 --> 00:12:30,064
Strange hardware without great software becomes a gimmick.

229
00:12:30,344 --> 00:12:34,790
Great software without control of the platform becomes dependent on somebody else's rules.

230
00:12:35,070 --> 00:12:38,501
Famous characters without reinvention become museum pieces.

231
00:12:39,221 --> 00:12:43,631
Nintendo has all three—and more than a century of institutional memory about

232
00:12:43,631 --> 00:12:45,952
what happens when a toy stops being fun.

233
00:12:48,972 --> 00:12:52,544
Nintendo began with paper, ink, and a deck of cards.

234
00:12:52,824 --> 00:12:56,578
That origin matters—not because playing cards predicted video games,

235
00:12:56,578 --> 00:12:59,559
but because they taught the company its oldest lesson.

236
00:13:00,279 --> 00:13:01,976
The object is not the entertainment.

237
00:13:02,256 --> 00:13:05,283
The entertainment is what happens between people,

238
00:13:05,283 --> 00:13:07,321
rules, imagination, and surprise.

239
00:13:08,041 --> 00:13:09,037
Consoles change.

240
00:13:09,317 --> 00:13:10,260
Screens change.

241
00:13:10,540 --> 00:13:11,780
Business models change.

242
00:13:12,060 --> 00:13:15,295
The industry repeatedly mistakes those changes for the point.

243
00:13:15,575 --> 00:13:17,959
Nintendo's history suggests something simpler.

244
00:13:18,679 --> 00:13:22,175
Technology matters most when it disappears into a moment:

245
00:13:22,175 --> 00:13:26,223
a perfectly timed jump, a discovery you were not supposed to find,

246
00:13:26,223 --> 00:13:28,064
a race lost by one blue shell,

247
00:13:28,064 --> 00:13:31,314
a controller handed to someone who was only watching.

248
00:13:32,034 --> 00:13:34,914
That is why Nintendo can look outdated and arrive early.

249
00:13:35,194 --> 00:13:37,727
Why it can fail dramatically and return stronger.

250
00:13:38,007 --> 00:13:42,188
Why a company founded in 1889 can still make the future feel playful.

251
00:13:42,908 --> 00:13:46,444
Nintendo does not endure because it always knows what comes next.

252
00:13:46,724 --> 00:13:50,793
